# Copy to `.env` (gitignored) and fill in — or let `bash scripts/setup-board.sh` do it for you.
# Never commit the real values: the conformance check scans every pull request for credentials,
# but it fires after the push — a committed key is burned and must be rotated first, then removed
# (CONTRIBUTING.md). Where each value comes from is `docs/credentials.md`.

# The CTFd board the Solver plays. This one value decides which competition it enters, so it is
# the guard against pointing a permitted-AI agent at a no-AI platform.
#   BrunnerCTF Global (AI permitted): https://global.brunnerctf.dk
#   BrunnerCTF Denmark — strict no-AI, never point the Solver here.
CTFD_URL=

# A CTFd access token minted on that board: Settings -> Access Tokens -> Generate.
# Shown once. It carries the account's full privileges, so it is per-board and revocable.
CTFD_API_TOKEN=

# This Run's identity, and the one value the Solver refuses to mint for itself. The Run's absolute
# deadline is stamped under /state/runs/<RUN_ID>, and it is consulted only when there is no window
# there already — which is what makes a restart inherit the deadline instead of extending it. A
# fresh id on every boot would hand a restarted Solver a fresh window, silently, with nobody there.
# So it comes from this file and never from a timestamp or a uuid generated at start.
RUN_ID=

# Optional. Shortens the Run's window and can never lengthen one, so a practice run can be half an
# hour without any environment variable being able to buy a Run past the event it is playing.
# Seconds. Delete the line for the whole window the board profile states.
# RUN_SECONDS=

# Optional. The model every rung of the credential chain runs at — v1 has one brain per Run and the
# chain switches on exhaustion alone (ADR-0014). Which ids a subscription serves is account state
# and it moves: `gpt-5` and `gpt-5-codex` are both refused on ours, so this is config here rather
# than a constant in the image. `python3 scripts/codex_probe.py` says whether the one you would
# spend a Run on answers, and lists what the login was offered. Defaults to gpt-daybreak-blue-latest.
# CODEX_MODEL=

# The IN-CYPHER team key. It identifies and gates this team on the competition platform —
# including the proof-of-work gate on raw-TCP challenges — and must stay within the team.
# Read from the IN-CYPHER board under Settings -> Access Tokens, where it is *displayed*: there is
# nothing to generate and, unlike the CTFd token above, nothing to revoke. It cannot be rotated
# from our side at all, which is why it is the one secret here whose leak we could not undo.
# Not needed for practice boards like Brunner — and it belongs in that board's overlay file rather
# than here, so a run pointed elsewhere carries no IN-CYPHER credential (docs/credentials.md).
TEAM_KEY=

# How the Solver pays for inference. Any LLM is allowed, and we hold subscriptions to more
# than one, so this is per provider rather than a single key. We compete on a SUBSCRIPTION;
# metered credit is break-glass only. Together these form a chain the Solver walks on its own
# when one is exhausted — subscription first, metered last — because a fallback that waits for
# a human to reach for it is the intervention the rules penalise (ADR-0010).
#
# The quota is the cap and it hard-blocks: Codex meters on a 5-hour rolling window AND a weekly
# cap, by tokens, across one pool shared with the CLI and cloud tasks. The scored run is 5.5
# hours, so your own usage on the day comes out of the Solver's headroom.
#
# ONE credential per provider. An empty value is not an unset one: the variable occupies its
# slot in the client's credential search the moment it exists, so a leftover
# `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=` shadows a token and authenticates with nothing — and `docker run
# --env-file` exports empty values, so the container fails while this file still looks right.
# Delete the lines you are not using rather than blanking them.

# Anthropic — subscription. `sk-ant-oat01-…`, minted by `claude setup-token`. Use
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN when the Solver drives Claude Code or the Agent SDK; use
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN when it calls the Messages API directly. Either way the token rides
# `Authorization: Bearer` — putting it in ANTHROPIC_API_KEY sends `x-api-key` and fails auth.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# Anthropic — metered, break-glass. `sk-ant-api03-…` from console.anthropic.com. Never set
# alongside a token: the SDK sends both headers and the API rejects the request outright.
# BELONGS IN THE SCORED BOARD'S OVERLAY, NOT HERE. The chain switches to metered on its own,
# so a practice run holding this key would quietly start spending real money the moment the
# subscription capped. Keep it out of `.env` and a practice run structurally cannot — same rule
# as TEAM_KEY, same reason (docs/credentials.md).
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# OpenAI / Codex — metered, break-glass. `sk-…` from platform.openai.com. Overlay only, for the
# reason directly above.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# OpenAI / Codex — metered, break-glass. Not a key: Codex authenticates by file, so this is the
# CODEX_HOME of a second login made with the metered account. OVERLAY ONLY, and that is the whole
# control — the chain switches to metered on its own, so a practice run that carried this would
# start spending real money the moment the subscription capped. Absent, there is no metered rung
# in the chain at all: no flag to set, no mode to remember (ADR-0010).
# CODEX_HOME_METERED=/state/codex-metered

# OpenAI / Codex — subscription. Nothing goes here, deliberately. Codex does NOT read a token from
# the environment: it stores credentials in auth.json under CODEX_HOME, and the container is
# authenticated by running `codex login --device-auth` inside it before the run starts, with
# CODEX_HOME pointed at the host-mounted /state so a restart inherits the login.
# `--with-access-token` is Enterprise-only, so there is no token to put in a variable here
# (ADR-0011). See docs/credentials.md.
